 "text": "Everyone building with AI keeps saying commit, push and branch. This is what those words actually mean. Here's the problem. Most people building with AI have one copy of their app, on one laptop, and every request rewrites it. So a prompt breaks something, and there's no way back to the version from an hour ago. Or the laptop dies, and the whole project goes with it. GitHub fixes both. Every commit is a snapshot of your entire project, so a bad prompt is one step back. Push sends that history online, so a dead computer costs you nothing. And when a version is solid, tag it v1, so you can always get back to it. A branch gives a risky idea its own copy to play in. If it works out, you merge it in, and if it flops, you throw it away. You don't type any of this yourself. Tell Claude to commit, push or make a branch, and it runs the commands. It's free, and over two hundred and twenty-five million developers work this way. Subscribe to learn more about AI.",
